WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.770
Film Courage: How does a filmmaker know how
much to spend in order to get their money

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:03.770
back?

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:06.080
Jeff Deverett, Film Producer, Entrepreneur,
Writer, Director: Great question.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:12.120
This is where filmmakers get upset with distributors
because they usually get screwed by distributors,

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.769
that’s the truth because distribution is
a whole different business mindset.

00:00:15.769 --> 00:00:21.110
They don’t have the sensitivity to filmmakers.

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:25.150
But distributors do serve a very good function
in the industry.

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:30.789
I mean they do know how much a movie is worth,
where to monetize it, where to sell it and

00:00:30.789 --> 00:00:34.390
they have a pretty good idea how much they’re
going to get for it.

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:40.710
The best way to sort of find out what the
range will be, will be through a distributor.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:45.060
Now there are sales agents who do these forecasts
for you.

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:46.719
They give you these ranges.

00:00:46.719 --> 00:00:51.470
Most indie films when they are trying to get
financed they will get a sales agent to do

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:52.470
a range.

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:54.040
I’ve seen so many of them.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:57.640
I don’t know if you’ve seen them but there
is the low, the middle and the high.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.320
Sometimes there are five of them right?

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:05.239
They’re forecasts based on other like films
or comparisons.

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:09.659
You have to be super careful that you’re
comparing them to like films in the same sort

00:01:09.659 --> 00:01:10.990
of time frame.

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:13.810
Sometimes they will compare to like films
that were released 10 years ago.

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:18.009
Well I mean 10 years ago was a completely
different market than it was today.

00:01:18.009 --> 00:01:23.100
So you want the comps to be as accurate as
possible but they will do these comparisons

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:28.109
and they will say the middle range is sort
of what you can expect and it sort of it what

00:01:28.109 --> 00:01:29.109
you can expect.

00:01:29.109 --> 00:01:31.319
These are people who do this for a living.

00:01:31.319 --> 00:01:37.399
They know where ultimately they are going
to sell it, where the market will bear, internationally

00:01:37.399 --> 00:01:43.060
and from each player and they do a forecast
that do give you a pretty good idea - not

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:46.880
dead on but at least a range and that is the
best way to gauge it.

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:52.579
Film Courage: So similar to how a realtor
would get comps…well this is a fixer and

00:01:52.579 --> 00:01:57.200
this is the square footage and so I think
your property will go for this or this one

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.570
you’ve just redone the kitchen and bathroom
and you’ve got a huge lot…similar?

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:03.869
Jeff: Correct, that’s the same thing.

00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:08.099
I mean I think real estate is a little bit
more predicable and accurate because it’s

00:02:08.099 --> 00:02:12.709
a confined area whereas film is the whole
world but it’s the same concept.

00:02:12.709 --> 00:02:16.159
Film Courage: Does that mean if I pick up
the phone and just call a distributor they

00:02:16.159 --> 00:02:18.760
will give me an idea of what my budget should
be?

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.579
Jeff: …Not necessarily.

00:02:20.579 --> 00:02:25.709
I mean we should talk a little bit about distributors
and how to find a distributor and who is reputable

00:02:25.709 --> 00:02:31.069
and who will give you the right answer assuming
they will even answer your phone call.

00:02:31.069 --> 00:02:35.439
There are…one you can pay for this, there
are services that provide that.

00:02:35.439 --> 00:02:38.540
They charge a lot and I think they’re a
bit of a rip off.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:44.099
I only think that because I do my own forecasts
based on my own market knowledge but if you

00:02:44.099 --> 00:02:48.890
don’t have any access to anything else,
you can pay for that and I forget there are

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:50.780
some big companies that actually do that.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:53.720
They provide forecasts and they do comp [comparable
sales] reports.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:56.400
You know they are fancy and they give you
the whole thing and you can take those to

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.620
investors and say I’ve got this whole report
done from this company who specializes in

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:02.590
this and they’re credible.

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:03.790
There are sales agents.

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:08.069
So there is a difference between a sales agent
and a distributor, I mean that line is sort

00:03:08.069 --> 00:03:11.409
of crossing over a little bit more.

00:03:11.409 --> 00:03:19.349
An agent will say this is…what they can
tell you what to expect and they are easier

00:03:19.349 --> 00:03:21.760
to get to than distributors.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:27.859
A distributor generally wants to look at the
movie when it’s finished and that’s why

00:03:27.859 --> 00:03:29.090
they’re harder to get to.

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:34.310
They don’t really want to look at the script
or the idea or be pitched on it.

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:40.109
They would be able to tell you and if you
can get to them (and we can talk a minute

00:03:40.109 --> 00:03:46.549
about how to get to them) then you can say
I want to make an $800,000 horror film and

00:03:46.549 --> 00:03:51.569
it’s going to have this many number of special
effects and no talent and they’ll say expect

00:03:51.569 --> 00:03:59.080
to expect to get $800,000 or a million…they
could say that off the cuff over coffee.

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:01.189
They don’t need to go and do analysis or
anything like that.

00:04:01.189 --> 00:04:04.409
These people know this and this is what they
do.

00:04:04.409 --> 00:04:06.269
So how do you find a distributor?

00:04:06.269 --> 00:04:08.659
How do you get an honest answer?

00:04:08.659 --> 00:04:14.200
It’s getting tougher and tougher to find
a reputable distributor.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:18.400
First of all that business is getting tougher.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:23.720
The world is changing, people’s viewing
habits are changing to consumption of movies

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:24.720
is changing.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:30.870
I mean a lot of it now is through the streamers,
through the subscription video-on-demand services,

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.440
the Netflixs, the Hulus, the Disney Pluses.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:35.210
It’s easy for consumers.

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:41.960
You’re spending $5.00 to $10.00 a month
and getting an incredible array of fantastic

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:42.960
product.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:47.840
It’s made movie making such a pleasure (and
television) and look at the quality of product

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:51.319
they’re getting on some of these, I mean
it’s incredible I mean the amount these

00:04:51.319 --> 00:04:56.250
streamers spend on product because it’s
a volume game, they can afford it so that’s

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:58.320
making distribution more difficult.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:04.069
So distributors are falling by the wayside
also so they are getting far and fewer between

00:05:04.069 --> 00:05:07.160
and they generally want to deal with bigger
budget product.

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:10.930
They’re not crazy about dealing with lower
budget project for the same reasons a real

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:18.070
estate agent is not as crazy about doing a
$100,000 sale than a $5,000,0000 sale, same

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:20.980
amount of work, much less commission.

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:25.759
So they want to deal with bigger stuff because
they will make more money off of it.

00:05:25.759 --> 00:05:31.800
But to find a reputable distributor, I mean
you start…there are different ways you can

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.259
go about it depending on who you are and what
your resources are.

00:05:34.259 --> 00:05:37.800
I mean you can go to a market like the American
Film Market [AFM] or something like that.

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:40.389
You can register for it, it’s not cheap.

00:05:40.389 --> 00:05:45.990
But that will give you access to all of the
distributors who are registered there plus

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:50.889
access to their guide Who Is Registered In
The American Film Marketing Association.

00:05:50.889 --> 00:05:54.560
You can do the same with Reed MIDEM who runs
MIPCOM [International Market of Communications

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:58.240
Programmes] or that type of thing.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:02.810
Like when you register as a buyer or seller
or whatever as an attendee for a major market

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:07.699
like that, half the value is going to the
market and you know hobnobbing and seeing

00:06:07.699 --> 00:06:12.789
what goes on and the other half is having
access to the database so now you have say

00:06:12.789 --> 00:06:21.720
with the case of MIPTV there are two to three
thousand distributors in that book and in

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:24.009
their database so now you have to contact
them.

00:06:24.009 --> 00:06:30.520
You have to reach out and it’s like any
type of networking, you try to be brief, you

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:35.210
explain that you’re a filmmaker making this
kind of movie, here is what it’s going to

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:39.449
be, dress it up a little bit, you’ve got
to sell yourself a little bit and say you

00:06:39.449 --> 00:06:44.889
just want to, you’re interested in taking
it to them after the movie is finished but

00:06:44.889 --> 00:06:49.280
you want to get an indication of sort of what
budget range you should be working in to make

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:54.210
sure that you’re not going to, that you
have a chance of actually recouping and some

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:55.569
of them will be cooperative.

00:06:55.569 --> 00:07:00.000
Like anything else in the world half of them
will be rude and the other ones you know.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.669
And then you just hopefully get lucky and
you get somebody on the phone who is nice

00:07:04.669 --> 00:07:07.770
and will give you…or not even on the phone
through an email or whatever the case may

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:12.449
be and if you can’t do it that way you are
going to have to pay to get a report.

00:07:12.449 --> 00:07:18.490
Film Courage: And with this report some of
the comps again special effects, no name talent,

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:20.139
what about locations?

00:07:20.139 --> 00:07:21.659
How many locations you plan to shoot?

00:07:21.659 --> 00:07:24.729
Things like that, does that factor in?

00:07:24.729 --> 00:07:29.280
Jeff: Everything factors in depending on who
you get because it’s also subjective but

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:31.289
it doesn’t really totally factor in.

00:07:31.289 --> 00:07:34.949
I mean the main component is talent.

00:07:34.949 --> 00:07:40.449
If you have a certain level of talent then
it elevates it into a different category see

00:07:40.449 --> 00:07:41.550
there are ranges.

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:46.189
It’s not like a total linear thing.

00:07:46.189 --> 00:07:49.819
I personally think and again this is just
my opinion right?

00:07:49.819 --> 00:07:56.889
But from my experience if you’re dealing
in low-budget indie films stay below about

00:07:56.889 --> 00:07:59.560
1.5 maximum.

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:05.789
I used to say two million but 1.5 maximum
because the market is getting tougher and

00:08:05.789 --> 00:08:08.389
tougher and tougher to monetize that.

00:08:08.389 --> 00:08:13.780
Between 1.5, if once you start getting into
that if you don’t have major talent like

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:19.990
at least somebody who is recognizable then
you’re better off jumping into 5 million.

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:24.360
Like between 1.5 and 5 is almost no man’s
land unless you can figure out how to get

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:25.520
talent in there.

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:29.180
You have a better chance of getting your money
back if you do say a 5 million dollar movie

00:08:29.180 --> 00:08:34.410
that has name talent then you do doing a 3
million dollar deal that’s really, really

00:08:34.410 --> 00:08:39.380
well done but it doesn’t have name talent
because talent sells.

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:42.212
Most distributors the first question they
ask is who is in it?

00:08:42.212 --> 00:08:47.310
And the reason they ask that is because most
consumers ask that because they say “Hey

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:48.610
I heard this movie, who is in it?”

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:52.230
That’s is 90% of what people ask is who
is in it.

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:55.350
And when they recognize somebody they might
take a chance with it.

00:08:55.350 --> 00:09:02.540
Film Courage: So if you do got to AFM or MIPCOM
or something like that then you would look

00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:04.960
at the distributors and make sure you’re
reaching out to the ones that are going to

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:05.960
be in your genre?

00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:08.740
Like don’t…if you have a romcom, don’t
go to an only horror [distributor]?

00:09:08.740 --> 00:09:11.600
Jeff: Yes and if you have a narrative don’t
go to a documentary distributor.

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:16.930
Some people only specialize in factual, some
in non-scripted, yes there are special…those

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:21.520
are all listed also, they are all categorized
people when they list…when the distributors

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:26.810
list in the databases they fill out a form
and say this is who were, this is what we

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:32.250
sell, this is what we specialize in, so try
to hone in on the distributor who would be

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:34.540
most suitable to what you’re making.

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:36.350
Film Courage: And keep the email brief?

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:43.250
Jeff: It’s funny, I am talking so much right
now but generally I write super brief emails

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:48.710
and I keep my correspondence super brief and
that is because we live in a world of super

00:09:48.710 --> 00:09:51.060
short attention spans.

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:52.280
People don’t like to read a lot.

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:56.630
Everything is sounds bites, everything is
super quick, everything is Twitter feeds and

00:09:56.630 --> 00:09:58.360
Instagram posts and stuff like that.

00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:06.680
It’s all super, super short and with younger
executives who are now used to all this technology

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:10.890
they just don’t want to read long emails
and don’t want to hear long pitches and

00:10:10.890 --> 00:10:13.270
all this kind of stuff, get to the point quickly.

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:14.270
Question for the viewers: Have you ever consulted
with a distributor before making a movie?

00:10:14.270 --> 00:10:14.277
Anything you can share about that process?

